(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "2609061400")
  (:adr/title "Recipient-bound disclosure grants over the encrypted CID plane")
  (:adr/status "proposed")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-06")
  (:adr/body "Implement recipient-bound disclosure as a signed key-delivery protocol over the existing ciphertext/CID plane. This proposal records the requested architecture and the tested first integration slice; it does not claim production deployment.\n\nDECISIONS\n1. Ciphertext identity is its raw CIDv1/SHA-256. Grant, request, wrapped-key envelope and delivery-event identities use kotoba.value.v1 canonical CIDs. Identical plaintext does not acquire a new identity merely because a different user decrypts it.\n2. An exact-resource grant binds tenant, owner, issuer, recipient, encryption-key fingerprint, policy CID, operations, parent grant CID, bounded delegation depth, time window and epoch. The issuer signs a domain-separated canonical payload. A child cannot widen operations/time or change resource/tenant/owner/policy/epoch; its issuer must be the parent's recipient.\n3. Publish the wrapped key's CID in the grant, not the wrapped key itself before delivery. An already public recipient-encrypted envelope is an offline bypass of any online audit gate. Public ciphertext storage and controlled wrapped-key delivery are distinct.\n4. Require a recipient-signed, audience-bound request with scoped nonce. Resolve keys using trusted principal/tenant/epoch/record-kind state. Request fields cannot establish owner or principal authority. Human identity bootstrap remains governed by existing Passkey-only policy.\n5. Before returning a key envelope, verify the whole bounded grant chain, apply existing local authority and individual revocation state, atomically consume the scoped nonce, sign a key-envelope-release-authorized event, durably commit it under its canonical CID, and reread it exactly. No mutable ref selects canonical truth.\n6. Ayatori consumes the service at Arrangement's existing decrypt-fn seam. It hashes the actual encrypted bytes, validates response/recipient/request/grant/epoch bindings, refreshes local authority after delivery and verifies the executor signature before calling qualified crypto. Ciphertext caches do not cache authority. Governed query visibility and execution receipts remain unchanged.\n7. Preserve kotoba.security.crypto-policy/check-production-envelope's hybrid-required contract. New code handles orchestration, not cryptographic primitives. The qualified provider must authenticate binding(grant) as info/AAD, bind the local secret key to the recipient-key fingerprint and authenticate decrypted content.\n8. Parent references are canonical CID strings under the application contract; they are not generic IPLD Link values. Owner-visible event discovery, retention, protected audit metadata and witness/checkpoint completeness need host integration.\n9. PRE is deferred until offline-owner requirements and per-object scope/collusion guarantees are specified. ABE/FE and fingerprinting/traitor tracing are separate profiles. No claim of recovering read data or identifying a leaked identical plaintext by its CID.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION\nkotoba-lang/kotobase PR #77: disclosure-grant release!/release-async!, response verification, exact shape/scope checks and a domain-separated grant/envelope protocol; docs/disclosure-grants.edn is the detailed interface and rollout inventory.\nkotoba-lang/ayatori: recipient-decryptor/recipient-decryptor-async at the existing remote cursor seam; dependent PR pins the new Kotobase commit and its canonical value codec.\nNeither changes live key custody, authentication ceremonies, runtime endpoints, main-branch fleet pins, or the query authority model.\n\nEVIDENCE (2026-09-06)\nnbb 1.5.212 / Node 24.19.0 with pinned dependencies.\nKotobase focused suite: 13 tests, 59 assertions, zero failures/errors. Includes actual Ed25519 signatures, tampering, parent/recipient substitution, operation/time amplification, replay, awaited commit/readback and owner-signed hybrid downgrade refusal.\nAyatori complete discovered suite: 107 tests, 324 assertions, zero failures/errors. Includes real AES-GCM encrypted Arrangement -> verified provider bytes -> audited key delivery -> decryption -> Datalog result. Key-envelope fixtures are explicitly synthetic and do not qualify a hybrid KEM provider.\nProvider qualification (2026-09-06, after the two PRs above): the ML-KEM-768 half of the hybrid provider is now qualified rather than asserted. kotoba-lang/envelope's envelope.qualify runs the deployed @noble/post-quantum module against known answers generated by a different implementation -- BouncyCastle 1.81, via kotoba-lang/security's conformance/crypto/vectors -- and hands the evidence to kotoba.security.crypto-policy/evaluate-pq-provider, which returns :pq-provider/qualified? true with no violations. Reproduce with `cd orgs/kotoba-lang/envelope && npm run qualify:ml-kem`. The module digest is pinned over the three package files that implement ML-KEM and refuses at the point of use on a mismatch; @noble/post-quantum moves from a caret range to an exact version, because a range leaves the deployed provider unmeasured. Empty vectors are refused rather than passed and a tampered known answer fails the check, both tested. envelope.sealed-key frames one hybrid wrap as the octet vector :sealed/ciphertext carries, with binding(grant) as the AEAD AAD; wrong binding, wrong recipient, substituted encapsulation, malformed frame and a flipped byte in each region are refused. envelope suite 66 tests / 179 assertions, zero failures, on nbb 1.4.208 / Node 26.7.0.\nConsumer wiring (2026-09-06): ayatori.disclosure-open/recipient-opener builds ayatori's :open! port on that provider. The content key exists once, is wrapped under binding(grant), travels as the grant's :sealed/ciphertext, and is recovered through the qualified provider or not at all -- before this the port took a closure that already held the key, which tested the delivery protocol and not the delivery of a key. A custody store that returns a key for a different fingerprint is refused by name rather than four steps later as an AEAD failure; an absent binding is refused rather than used; the recovered key reaches the content decryptor and nothing else and is overwritten after (best effort, and said so). Ayatori suite 131 tests / 399 assertions, zero failures, on nbb 1.5.212 / Node 26.7.0, with the classpath in bin/classpath.txt. Each refusal was shown to discriminate: removing it turned exactly one test red, and the right one.\nCustody (2026-09-06): envelope.keystore holds a hybrid recipient as a record -- public material, a :key/status and two wraps, one per half, each sealed under an AAD carrying the recipient's fingerprint -- and the fingerprint is DERIVED from the two public keys rather than assigned, so a grant's :recipient-key is something the issuer and the custodian compute the same way from the same bytes rather than a label they agree to use. Ayatori's :keys! port is envelope.keystore/unlocker over those records. Refusals, each shown to discriminate: unknown fingerprint, a :key/status that is not :active (the test iterates kotoba.security.key-status's own blocked set rather than a copy), a relabelled or edited record, the two halves exchanged, a wrong unlock secret, and an ML-KEM secret whose embedded encapsulation key disagrees with the record. Absent and revoked reject differently -- an audit that cannot tell them apart cannot see a revocation take effect. Envelope suite 78 tests / 203 assertions; Ayatori suite 137 / 407; zero failures in both.\nOne test-runner defect was found and fixed while doing this, and it is the reason the numbers above are trustworthy: seven of twelve deftest forms in a new file were swallowed into a preceding form by one unbalanced paren, and the suite reported zero failures over the five that survived. Both repositories' runners now refuse (exit 2) when a file declares more top-level deftest forms than its namespace registers, and report how many files carry reader conditionals and therefore cannot be counted that way.\nNo production durable store, live endpoint, fleet run, JVM suite or native/Wasm whole-component acceptance is claimed. GitHub Actions are not the CI authority. No key custody, no FIPS validation and no side-channel certification are claimed -- evaluate-pq-provider returns the last two as :pq-provider/non-claims itself.\n\nROLLOUT GAPS\nP1 (2026-09-06): the hybrid KEM provider is qualified, the consumer is wired and custody has a custodian; see EVIDENCE. What remains under this gate is DEPLOYMENT rather than implementation -- which authenticator supplies the unlock secret is a property of a deployment, not of a library (envelope.passkey describes the WebAuthn PRF path and nothing wires it), and it therefore overlaps the P1 delivery-host gap below rather than adding library work here. Everything this gate names that can be verified in a repository is verified. That sentence is the one that turns into \"it is done\" when quoted one step later, so: nothing here is deployed, no key service exists, and the gate is open.\nP1: wire trusted signing/encryption-key identity, policy/revocation and atomic nonce state plus canonical durable delivery events at a real service; verify owner observation.\nP2: register/run the fleet gate and whole-component native/Wasm Q9 qualification before consumer cutover.\nP2: measure per-object hashing, signature and durable-write/read costs before batching; a batching protocol must preserve exact resource coverage.\nPR merge is separate from deployment. Resume at the linked adapter contract; adopt provider and storage implementations before enabling the service.")))
